{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj; sed -n '/### 1.4/,/### 1.5/p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md; cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792630000000-reviewer-8ab-descriptors.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\"","aggregated_output":";; Run from the repository root: clojure -M collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj\n;; The adjacent EDN file prints every intermediate; no lowerer or private var\n;; is needed. Projection fingerprints use printed map ASTs.\n(require '[clojure.edn :as edn]\n         '[clojure.string :as str]\n         '[dao.jing :as jing]\n         '[dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n         '[yin.vm :as vm]\n         '[yin.vm.debruijn :as db]\n         '[yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dc]\n         '[yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode])\n\n(def intermediates\n  (edn/read-string (slurp \"collab/1792620000000-8ab-intermediates.edn\")))\n\n(defn recompute [{:keys [kind input]}]\n  (case kind\n    :h (dc/image-hash input)\n    :r (rcode/register-hash input)\n    :descriptor (jing/blake3 (dc/encode-scalar input))\n    :dimension (jing/blake3 (db/encode-value input))\n    :projection (:fingerprint (db/project-datoms (vm/ast->datoms input)))\n    :segment (jing/segment-key input)))\n\n(assert (= dc/descriptor (get-in intermediates [\"44c761ee3ba9b5669385841b361b76f86f0b1db7b3b42f1c7966458669f6361e\" :input])))\n(assert (= rcode/descriptor\n           (get-in intermediates\n                   [\"7eacba41f9f1548d18b724cbd2db87945f09ec7b4ab2f303399e9b6497007632\" :input])))\n(assert (= db/descriptor\n           (get-in intermediates\n                   [\"1f4ec95cdfd666cf5da2be5f47fd3a7e3c942dfa2ea87e61e0062270f61a0803\" :input])))\n\n(doseq [[old entry] (sort-by key intermediates)]\n  (when (str/starts-with? old \"handoff-v2:\")\n    (let [[_ address & hex] (str/split-lines\n                             (slurp \"test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/handoff-v2.txt\"))\n          encoded (apply str (map #(format \"%02x\" (bit-and 255 %))\n                                  (cbor/encode (:input entry))))]\n      (assert (= address (subs (str (recompute entry)) 1)))\n      (assert (= (apply str hex) encoded))))\n  (prn [old (recompute entry)]))\n### 1.4 Every pinned H, R, descriptor hash and A address\n\n| Constant | File:line | What it pins | Phase-8 action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 H + 1 R pair | `semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:240` | §6.3 row 6 image hashes of the new lowerer | regenerate (descriptor + BLAKE3) |\n| 10 R hashes | `derive_test.cljc:419-428` | `register-goldens` | regenerate |\n| 1 R descriptor hash `7eacba41…` | `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc:7` (already moved at 5b; duplicate at `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:446` dies) | `rcode/descriptor-hash` | regenerate |\n| H descriptor hash | `debruijn_code_test.cljc:368` is still `(is (string? dc/descriptor-hash))` | NOT pinned | **pin at 8a before anything else** (§10 item 8 precondition) |\n| H `52791d4a…`, R `c0aefe2f…` | `linker_test.cljc:522-531` (`pinned-identities-are-host-independent`), helpers `stack-image`/`register-image` `:253-260` use `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` | the one host-independence golden | helpers switch to `derive`, both strings regenerate |\n| 12 R hashes/images | `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:449-620` | old lowerer | deleted, not regenerated (§8.1) |\n| 6 node/dimension hashes | `debruijn_test.cljc:136, :933, :1300-1306` | projection `dimension-hash`, node hashes (SHA-256) | regenerate under BLAKE3 (surviving hashes move) |\n| 6 image hashes + 2 hex images | `debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc:321-347` | old `lower-stack` output and H of corpus rows (incl. `52791d4a…`) | the images are the old lowerer's; either regenerate from `derive/stack-image` (if bytes equal) or delete the byte pins and keep behavioural parity (recommend: regenerate, and the test's producer becomes `derive`) |\n| `manifest-golden` `b9dbaeff…` | `yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:223-235` | py manifest address (JVM golden) | regenerate (C4 file, Q1) |\n| `pysp-manifest-golden` `0971cc1e…` | `linked_safepoint_test.cljc:240-247` | pysp manifest | regenerate (C4 file, Q1) |\n| 5 + 1 segment addresses | `int_literal_test.cljc` (5), `float_address_test.cljc:227-269` (1 + 4 fragments) | A addresses of Python literals under the OLD v3 vector? To verify at 8f: if these are tree or v4 addresses they hold; if v3 vector addresses they die with v3 | verify, regenerate only if they are v3 (C4 files, Q1) |\n| 32 A addresses | `semantic_register/corpus.cljc` | v4 vector addresses | must NOT change; a gate, not a regeneration |\n| 20 addresses | `ucf/rows_test.cljc` | pack roots / A | must NOT change |\n| 2 | `yang/safepoint_test.cljc` | to verify | as above |\n| scanner goldens | `semantic_register/scanner_goldens.cljc` (7231 lines, v3 answers at 993d5411) | historical evidence | stays; the live comparisons in `scan_test.cljc:65-67, :188, :277-300` against `old-linearize`/`old-code` are deleted, `footprint-mnemonics-is-the-v3-table-minus-push` `:261-264` (`code/mnemonics`) is rewritten to compare against the pinned v3 mnemonic set as data |\n| manifests' `:yin.module/index` | `publish.cljc:189` `{h h-address r r-address}` | | new shape `{:yin.debruijn.code h-addr :yin.debruijn.register r-addr}` (derivations §8.1) |\n\nFixtures that would regenerate only if A moves: `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`, `ledger-v2.txt` (both 7a/7b). Gate: `(= (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)) <before>)` is unaffected by phase 8 because the v4 vector, `sr-code/contract \"v4\"` and `jing/segment-key` are untouched; the rename changes no hashed content (the keyword `:yin.semantic-register/code` is not in any pack: hex grep 0 hits).\n\n### 1.5 The old transport paths and who exercises them\n[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"muse-spark-1.3-contributor\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\nVerification complete. All eight brief items checked against repository evidence; two blocking findings. Delivering the review:\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-14 09:40:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-14 16:40:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 71ff60b2-6c20-4e6c-9a77-6cfced87ce56\n\n# Phase-8ab descriptor + BLAKE3 review: `67b98907..b794e2f5` (16 tracked files)\n\n**Merge verdict: NOT ready to merge into `srvm-phase8`.** Two blocking findings: one in-scope P1 regression (a provably red test the slice should have regenerated), one P2 in claimed acceptance (the 3-host gate). Everything else in the slice verifies clean against repo evidence — no new architectural defect found, and the report's deviations all check out. Fixes are small (<1 day).\n\n## Findings\n\n**P1 | `test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.edn:2,6` | evidence | concrete fix**\nThe artifact still pins the OLD py manifest `:segment/blake3-b9dbaeff80b3…` (line 2) and the OLD signed envelopes over OLD manifest addresses (line 6: `pym.m` `eef33c8c…`, `pym.k` `1221aa01…`, `pym.t` `5d12f25c…`), while this slice moved py to `577aabfb8632…` (proven by `linked_prelude_test.cljc` golden change in this same diff) and every H/R embedded in the guest blobs. Two assertions in the untouched consumer must now fail: `dht_import_test.cljc:370-378` (`this-host-publishes-the-artifact` — this host's `fixture/generate` vs the committed bytes) and `dht_import_test.cljc:397-398` (`(= (:py artifact) (:address @h/published))`). The repo itself prescribes the fix: `dht-import-v1.generate.clj:19-20` says \"Rerun it whenever the prelude, the lowering or the linker's publication moves an address; `dht-import-test` fails until it is.\" The engineer brief item (4) explicitly scoped \"any manifest/fixture golden that embeds an H or R\" into 8ab, and the old py address is in the report's own table — a grep for it would have found this file. The miss is contained: I grepped the full new tree for all old H/R/manifest prefixes (`52791d4a`, `c0aefe2f`, `e971cd9b`, `afe9e3ac`, `b9dbaeff`, `0971cc1e`) and this edn is the sole leftover. **Fix:** run the generator on the JVM per its header (`clojure … -M:t test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.generate.clj`, local-only, no network — committed packets + fixed seeds), commit the rewritten edn in this slice, and run `dht_import_test` including the slow mesh leg (needs real loopback, cf. the report's socket note).\n\n**P2 | (verification gap — no single line; claimed acceptance) | evidence | concrete fix**\nNo Node or Dart lane ran: report Verification table states \"no commit, Node lane or Dart lane was run.\" Cross-host agreement of the new BLAKE3 pins is therefore unproven, and it is claimed acceptance three ways: engineer brief \"Portability: `.cljc` for JVM/Node/Dart\"; `stack_parity_test.cljc:14-17` (\"committed literals asserted on every host lane, which is B5's cross-runtime identity check\"); architect §3 gate list (\"three hosts at every landing from 8ab up\"). Mitigating evidence I verified: `jing/blake3` has explicit per-host implementations (`dao/jing.cljc:114-135`, `:clj`/`:cljs`/`:cljd`) and BLAKE3 was already the Jing default exercised on all hosts — so residual risk is narrow (host-specific encoder divergence, e.g. the known CLJS double-provenance split the parity suite already branches on). **Fix:** run Node + Dart quick lanes over the nine touched test namespaces plus the `handoff-v2-census-test` before merging to `srvm-phase8`; after the P1 regen, run the `dht-import` slow test on all three hosts (its docstring at `dht_import_test.cljc:9-21` requires every host to arrive at the JVM artifact).\n\n**P3 | `test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc:169` | evidence | concrete fix**\nTest message still reads \"adapt + stack VM agrees with lower + semantic VM\" but pipeline A is now `derive/stack-image` (`image-of` at :94-96, `debruijn-outcome` docstring at :142 corrected). Cosmetic only. **Fix:** reword to \"derive + stack VM agrees …\".\n\n**P3 | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:1092,1094` | evidence | concrete fix**\nThe §8.1 table still cites the pre-8ab H/R prefixes (`52791d4a…`, `c0aefe2f…`) and `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` helper refs as the live check, and \"the twelve R hashes\" where the code has 11 (implementer already reported the inventory error). The mechanical-check prose remains valid, so this is doc staleness only. **Fix:** acceptable in the 8g docs sweep — annotate 8ab completion with the new prefixes and correct \"twelve\" → \"eleven\".\n\n## The eight brief items, with evidence\n\n1. **Two-row descriptor change, versions pinned — CONFIRMED.** HEAD vs new forms compared directly: H descriptor drops `[:… :dim/lift-to [:yin.code/*]]` and flips `:hash :sha256→:blake3` (`debruijn_code.cljc:128-141`); R descriptor identical shape of change (`debruijn_register_code.cljc:196-…`). `lowering-contract-version` is still `2` (`debruijn_code.cljc:109-114`, value line untouched) and `contract-version` still `4` (value untouched; only its docstring now cites ruling 2). (`debruijn.cljc`'s own descriptor correctly keeps `:dim/lift-from`/`projection-to` — only its `:dim/encoding` row changed, `:hash` plus the `:node-hash` prose, which is the Q4 second-row change, not a lift row.)\n2. **Six BLAKE3 sites, no SHA-256 path — CONFIRMED.** `jing/blake3` at `debruijn_code.cljc:542,558`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:258,270`, `debruijn.cljc:966,1028`; `:hash :blake3` rows at `debruijn_code.cljc:133`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:201`, `debruijn.cljc:139`; docstrings rewritten (H/R/node formulas). `grep -rn sha256|SHA-256` over all three source files returns nothing; calls are plain (no reader conditionals) and `jing/blake3` dispatches per host (`dao/jing.cljc:128-135`). SHA-256 correctly remains for the DHT routing contract (classification doc) and the Jing registry.\n3. **Regeneration through public functions — CONFIRMED by 5 end-to-end spot checks** (brief allowed mental rerun + spot checks; I did not execute per the read-only instruction). Each recompute fn verified public (`defn`, not `defn-`): `dc/image-hash` (:545), `rcode/register-hash` (:261), `jing/blake3` (jing:128), `dc/encode-scalar` (:418), `db/encode-value` (debruijn.cljc:902), `db/project-datoms` (:1103), `vm/ast->datoms` (vm.cljc:882), `jing/segment-key` (jing:182). Checked: H-descriptor `44c761ee…→7b7c6ae9…` pins in `debruijn_code_test`; dimension `1f4ec95c…→4b69e00d…` pins in `debruijn_test`; linker H `52791d4a…→d07cb2e9…`; py manifest `b9dbaeff…→577aabfb…` from the full printed manifest map; handoff `afe9e3ac…→52c7efaa…` plus the regen script's exact-CBOR-bytes assertion (`regen.clj:35-40`), with the script's descriptor-equality asserts (:25-31). Derive-test is 10H+10R (not the plan's 11 — plan-inventory error, code confirmed 10+10 in the diff); the 4 extra `debruijn_code_test` goldens, 4 parity rows with wire bytes kept, and linker goldens via `derive` all match table rows. The 51-row table matches the 51-entry claim (50 distinct new values; linker-R `bc069145…` correctly equals the new lowerer's worked-example R — different from the old allocator's `70de2f46…`, as it should be). The byte-identical-rerun claim itself is unverified by execution, but the script is deterministic by inspection (`sort-by key`, pure recompute, no time/net/randomness).\n4. **No A address moved — CONFIRMED.** `git diff --quiet` passes for `corpus.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, `layout_test.cljc`, `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`, `ledger-v2.txt`; the safepoint harness is absent from the 16-file diff. This satisfies the unitarity invariant (`datom.world.md:24-32`: the AST is never lost; derived forms are reinterpretations) as operationalized for phase 8 — the changed segment addresses (py/pysp/handoff) are content addresses whose *content* embeds regenerated H/R, not moved identities.\n5. **Kernels intact, `encode-scalar` in place — CONFIRMED.** `stack.cljc`/`register.cljc` absent from the diff; `encode-scalar` still `defn` at `debruijn_code.cljc:418`, still referenced by `debruijn_register_code` (`:scalar-encoder` data ref + encoder calls) and still tested in place. No widening, no removal, no dangling reference — the report's hazard analysis is correct.\n6. **C4 goldens correctly derived; 8c regen dependency REAL — CONFIRMED.** Both spot-checked end-to-end (item 3). The 8ab manifests still carry `:yin.semantic/code \"v3\"` and the old `{H addr R addr}` index shape (intermediates py entry), while `derivations.md:1095` documents the 8c change to `\"v4\"` plus the `{:yin.debruijn.code … :yin.debruijn.register …}` index reshape — so 8c necessarily regenerates them again, exactly as the report claims.\n7. **Handoff fixture — CONFIRMED.** The 18+/18- diff is the address line plus six hunks swapping the 64-char ASCII H (`e971cd9b…`→`688b2b45…`) inside CBOR text fields; no other bytes change. The plan's \"no H/R in old fixtures\" was wrong for this file (raw-hex grep misses ASCII-in-CBOR) and the report's explanation is accurate. Fixture still present, dies in 8d per plan. No test pins the old address (grep: zero hits).\n8. **Old compiler test — CONFIRMED as coverage-preserving.** 11 pins regenerated (counted in diff), requires and the `(adapted …)` producer untouched (no require hunk), duplicated descriptor golden deleted. Same assertions, new hashes, no shim — correct interim posture until the planned 8e deletion. Related: the pairing verifier staying on the old allocator is also correct — `linker_test.cljc` scopes `rc/adapt` to the local `old-register-image` in the one authentic-pairing test while all general helpers use `derive`; production `linker.cljc` is untouched (absent from diff), so no 8c semantic leaks in early.\n\n## Method limits\n\nRead-only per the brief: I ran no tests, linters, or the regen script. The report's test/lint/mutant numbers (250 quick + 17 slow focused; 1149 broad; kondo clean; 4/4 mutants caught with sound cp-diff-restore methodology) are taken as reported, except where contradicted by evidence — the P1 (a suite member outside all reported gates is provably red) and the P2 (the reported gates are JVM-only where the phase gate requires three hosts). `target/8ab/` logs live outside the worktree and were not inspected.\n\n**Bottom line:** the hash move itself is correct, complete, and invariant-preserving; the deviations are all legitimate and evidenced. Unblock by regenerating `dht-import-v1.edn` and running the Node/Dart lanes, then merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git log -3 --oneline; rg -n '577aab|6c39c4|golden|image-hash|descriptor-hash' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_{prelude,safepoint}_test.cljc test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc; ls target; printenv | rg -i 'thread|session|codex'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git log -3 --oneline; rg -n '577aab|6c39c4|golden|image-hash|descriptor-hash' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_{prelude,safepoint}_test.cljc test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc; ls target; printenv | rg -i 'thread|session|codex'\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n6e38c90f refactor(yin.vm.debruijn): descriptors lose :dim/lift-to and the surviving H, R and descriptor hashes use BLAKE3, every pinned constant regenerated once (phase 8ab)\n2c70bec1 docs(orchestrator-log): semantic-register-vm seat handoff, phase 7 landed, phase 8 slices 8ab and 8d in flight\n67b98907 Merge branch 'srvm-phase7': custody on rows, the rows inspector, the holder's export and recovery format 2, composition and fencing over row packs, Stage E over the semantic-register kernel, phase 7 documentation\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:11:   golden fixture below -- see the final report).\"\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:117:      (is (not= (dc/image-hash composed) (dc/image-hash decomposed))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:137:         (is (not= (dc/image-hash (const-return 1/2)) (dc/image-hash (const-return 2)))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:158:       (is (not= (dc/image-hash (const-return 1)) (dc/image-hash (const-return 1.0)))))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:168:       (is (not= (dc/image-hash (const-return \\a)) (dc/image-hash (const-return \"a\")))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:177:       (is (not= (dc/image-hash (const-return 1/2)) (dc/image-hash (const-return \"1/2\")))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:205:  (testing \"image-hash refuses it inside a :const operand\"\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:207:           (unsupported-value-rule #(dc/image-hash (const-return lone-surrogate))))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:212:  (testing \"image-hash also refuses it inside a :str operand (P1-1's second\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:216:           (unsupported-value-rule #(dc/image-hash [[:gensym lone-surrogate] [:return]])))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:259:   bigint. `image-hash` values are pinned below in the golden-corpus\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:283:(def ^:private golden-corpus\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:295:(def ^:private golden-expected\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:296:  \"[encoded-image-hex image-hash], by the same keys as `golden-corpus`.\"\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:297:  ;; The image-hash literals below changed in the debruijn-b1-fix review\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:299:  ;; :image-hash formula string and the lift-morphism sentence), so\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:301:  ;; forked descriptor-hash, and therefore every H here, exactly once.\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:318:(deftest golden-corpus-bytes-and-hash\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:319:  (doseq [[k image] golden-corpus]\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:321:      (let [[expected-bytes expected-hash] (get golden-expected k)]\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:322:        (is (nil? (dc/image-defect image)) \"every golden fixture is itself valid\")\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:324:        (is (= expected-hash (dc/image-hash image)))))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:360:  (is (= \"7b7c6ae92e5fe53577718b615a3ed0bdd81796bf8074efe8c07d7005c45f3489\" dc/descriptor-hash))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:223:(def ^:private manifest-golden\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:235:                \"577aabfb863288b6f7aefbbb4c299befdf4825953aa7ccaa49a55428e26517ee\")))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:246:    (testing \"one address on every host (JVM golden)\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:247:      (is (= manifest-golden address) (pr-str (dissoc result :links :walk))))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:310:(def ^:private program-root-golden\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:345:              golden)\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:346:      (is (= program-root-golden root) (str root)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:240:(def ^:private pysp-manifest-golden\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:246:                \"6c39c4b94a086de50cff3bc780a9159870d5f1ef4aed47ad2f09846bc500bcf0\")))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:253:    (testing \"one address on every host (JVM golden)\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:254:      (is (= pysp-manifest-golden address) (pr-str (dissoc result :links))))\n8ab\ncompose-no-repl.clj\ntest-compose-5815fe7c-8cef-4cba-8d5a-c97fae6cb929\ntest-compose-9fc83268-d5f6-405b-8631-86e869ab0376\ntest-dht-local-d77caac5-1fd4-4b39-96b8-7b56acdc6be1.log\ntest-dht-local-db5ca7d5-fdb0-4f84-850e-50cb61d94048.log\nCLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION=1\nCLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ATTENDED=1\nCLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID=f14353d2-d973-4a48-a90b-8c33c81c9ab8\nCODEX_APPLY_PATCH_PRESERVE_LINE_ENDINGS=1\nCODEX_CI=1\nCODEX_MANAGED_BY_NPM=1\nCODEX_MANAGED_PACKAGE_ROOT=/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex\nCODEX_SANDBOX=seatbelt\nCODEX_SANDBOX_NETWORK_DISABLED=1\nCODEX_SESSION_ID=01a13b4b-763f-7c10-8ef4-9b01b60d6976\nCODEX_THREAD_ID=01a13b4b-763f-7c10-8ef4-9b01b60d6976\nCODEX_VERSION=0.162.1\nPATH=/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-darwin-arm64/vendor/aarch64-apple-darwin/codex-path:/Users/sto/.antigravity-ide/antigravity-ide/bin:/Users/sto/.opencode/bin:/Users/sto/.kimi-code/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/android-sdk/1.0/platform-tools:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/android-sdk/1.0/emulator:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/java/21.0.2/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/java/17.0.2/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/cljstyle/0.17.642:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/zprint/1.3.0:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/babashka/1.13.223:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/clojure/1.12.4.1602/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/node/25.6.1/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/android-sdk/23.0/cmdline-tools/23.0/bin:/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/flutter/3.47.6-stable/bin:/Users/sto/.antigravity/antigravity/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/System/Cryptexes/App/usr/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/local/bin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/bin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/appleinternal/bin:/pkg/env/global/bin:/Library/Apple/usr/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/Users/sto/.antigravity/antigravity/bin:/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/bin:/Users/sto/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg0Q9Csdc:/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-darwin-arm64/vendor/aarch64-apple-darwin/codex-path:/Users/sto/.antigravity-ide/antigravity-ide/bin:/Users/sto/.opencode/bin:/Users/sto/.kimi-code/bin:/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/bin\nSECURITYSESSIONID=186af\nTERM_SESSION_ID=AF702B04-01A8-4BE4-8C3E-4C54FCAF4B1F\n__MISE_ORIG_PATH=/Users/sto/.antigravity/antigravity/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/bin:/Users/sto/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/System/Cryptexes/App/usr/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/local/bin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/bin:/var/run/com.apple.security.cryptexd/codex.system/bootstrap/usr/appleinternal/bin:/pkg/env/global/bin:/Library/Apple/usr/bin\n__MISE_\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '215,255p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc; sed -n '235,258p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc; head -75 test/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc; head -90 test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc; ls target/8ab; rg -n 'published|publish|pysp|defn' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc; rg -n 'antlr-gen|antlr' deps.edn; git merge-base HEAD origin/master; git log origin/master -3 --oneline\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '215,255p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc; sed -n '235,258p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc; head -75 test/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc; head -90 test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc; ls target/8ab; rg -n 'published|publish|pysp|defn' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc; rg -n 'antlr-gen|antlr' deps.edn; git merge-base HEAD origin/master; git log origin/master -3 --oneline\"","aggregated_output":"          (str depth \" deep\")))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; A1 and A10. `py` publishes to one pinned manifest address; the walker is\n;; excluded, visibly\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private manifest-golden\n  \"I1 moved this once: the definition list gained the module runtime\n   (`py/import`, module objects, sys.modules and its two pre-seeded\n   modules) and the `ImportError` class. I2 moved it once: py/import-sub,\n   py/import-from, py/import-star, py/import-fail and their helpers, and\n   a top-level module's `__package__` ''. I3 moved it once:\n   py/star-item, `__all__` read by index (ranges included), and\n   py/run-session, the REPL `__main__` runner. It moved once more with\n   the rebase onto master 4529b5bd: the profile digest in the manifest\n   uses BLAKE3 since 492ff50b. Phase 8ab regenerates the H/R\n   checksums, derivation records and index keys.\"\n  (keyword (str \"segment/blake3-\"\n                \"577aabfb863288b6f7aefbbb4c299befdf4825953aa7ccaa49a55428e26517ee\")))\n\n\n(def ^:private vector-formats\n  [:yin.semantic/code :yin.debruijn.code :yin.debruijn.register])\n\n\n(defn- manifest-address\n  []\n  (let [{:keys [store result address]} @h/published\n        links (:links result)]\n    (testing \"one address on every host (JVM golden)\"\n      (is (= manifest-golden address) (pr-str (dissoc result :links :walk))))\n    (testing \"the three vector formats link :ok under :verifying\"\n      (doseq [f vector-formats]\n        (is (= :ok (get-in links [f :status])) (str f))\n        (is (= :verified (get-in links [f :trust])) (str f))))\n    (testing \"publishing twice into one store answers one address\"\n      (is (= address\n             (:address (publish/publish-module!\n                         store (prelude/module-spec registry))))))))\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; A1. `pysp` publishes to one pinned manifest address\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private pysp-manifest-golden\n  \"I1, I2 and I3 each moved this once: the pinned `py` requirement is\n   py's own manifest address, which each slice's prelude change moved,\n   and the BLAKE3 profile digest of 492ff50b moved again. Phase 8ab\n   regenerates H/R and the pinned py requirement.\"\n  (keyword (str \"segment/blake3-\"\n                \"6c39c4b94a086de50cff3bc780a9159870d5f1ef4aed47ad2f09846bc500bcf0\")))\n\n\n(defn- pysp-manifest-address\n  []\n  (let [{:keys [store result address]} @h/published-pysp\n        links (:links result)]\n    (testing \"one address on every host (JVM golden)\"\n      (is (= pysp-manifest-golden address) (pr-str (dissoc result :links))))\n    (testing \"the three vector formats link :ok under :verifying\"\n      (doseq [f [:yin.semantic/code :yin.debruijn.code\n                 :yin.debruijn.register]]\n        (is (= :ok (get-in links [f :status])) (str f))))\n(ns yang.python.antlr.linked-safepoint-test\n  \"C4 slice P3 (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6): the hook prelude as the\n   linker module `pysp`. The module emitter derives one module tree, its\n   exports and its publication spec from the same definition list the\n   bundled hook prelude loads; the spec declares every free name the tree\n   reads but the `py/*` exports, which its pinned `py` requirement covers.\n\n   Safepoint slice 1's signal acceptance and slice 2's recursion acceptance\n   re-run under the linked profile on the three vector VMs (the walker's\n   link stays refused until L-b), with the lowering's site marks preserved\n   through the stage, the stage's input carrying no prelude row, and a\n   derived program with no `pysp` binding failing closed on the unresolved\n   hook name. Both modules are `yang.python.antlr.linked-harness`'s: one\n   publication per process, served `:trusted`, so every test that publishes\n   or links is slow.\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.test-slow :as slow]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yang.python.antlr.linked-harness :as h]\n            [yang.python.antlr.lower :as lower]\n            [yang.python.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\n            [yang.python.antlr.render :as render]\n            [yang.python.antlr.safepoint :as hooks]\n            [yang.python.antlr.safepoint-programs :as programs]\n            [yang.python.antlr.uast :as u]\n            [yang.safepoint :as safepoint]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.data :as data]\n            [yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(defn- refusal-of\n  \"The ex-data of what `thunk` throws, or nil when it returns.\"\n  [thunk]\n  (try (thunk)\n       nil\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n         (or (ex-data e) {}))))\n\n\n(defn- def-key\n  \"The literal key of a `(yin/def k v)` application node, else nil.\"\n  [node]\n  (when (and (map? node)\n             (= :application (:type node))\n             (= 'yin/def (get-in node [:operator :name])))\n    (get-in node [:operands 0 :value])))\n\n\n(defn- nodes\n  \"Every node of map AST `ast`.\"\n  [ast]\n  (tree-seq map?\n            (fn [n] (filter map? (mapcat #(if (vector? %) % [%]) (vals n))))\n            ast))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The linked stage: marks in, no prelude rows, hooks out\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- envelope\n  \"The lowering's linked program batch for `pk`.\"\n  [pk]\n  (let [[_ [[port e]]] (lower/lower-transform {:prelude :linked} pk)]\n    (assert (= :program port))\n    e))\n\n\n(defn- derive*\n  \"`{:projected p :derived e' :record r}` for `pk` under `profile`.\"\n(ns yang.python.antlr.linked-harness\n  \"The linked profile's composition for the Python tests (C4 slices P2\n   and P3, docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6): the namespace that requires\n   this is the publisher of `py` and `pysp`, so it publishes each module\n   once per process into one in-memory content store and serves them to\n   every linked run over a `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under\n   `:trusted` derivation (the remediation ruling's R6). The `:verifying`\n   evidence is `publish-module!`'s own `link-local` links, which\n   `linked-prelude-test` asserts.\n\n   A linked run builds the program's task on one of the three vector VMs\n   with a link pair, then runs the VM and serves the pair alternately\n   until the VM stops blocking, as the REPL's drive does. The AST walker\n   is not a backend here: its link of `py` is refused `:undeclared-free`\n   until L-b.\"\n  (:require\n    [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n    [yang.python.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\n    [yang.python.antlr.safepoint :as safepoint]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.data :as data]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\n    [yin.vm.integer :as integer]\n    [yin.vm.linearize :as linearize]\n    [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\n    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n    [yin.vm.module :as module]\n    [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\n    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(def integer-limits\n  \"The ordinary Python composition's `integer` limits.\"\n  {::integer/max-bits 100000, ::integer/max-digits 4300})\n\n\n(defn registry\n  \"The full host registry a linked Python composition registers: the\n   `require` handler, then `cell`, `data`, `integer` under `limits`, and\n   `stream` — the hook module `pysp` polls a signal stream, so the\n   safepointed composition needs it even though `py` does not.\"\n  ([] (registry integer-limits))\n  ([limits]\n   (-> (module/default-registry)\n       module/register-cell-module\n       (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n       (prelude/register-integer-module limits)\n       module/register-stream-module)))\n\n\n(def published\n  \"`py` published once into its own content store:\n   `{:store s :result r :address a}`, `r` `publish-module!`'s answer.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [store (mem/create-content-mem)\n          result (publish/publish-module! store\n                                          (prelude/module-spec (registry)))]\n      {:store store, :result result, :address (:address result)})))\n\n\n(def published-pysp\n  \"`pysp` published once into `py`'s own content store, which its pinned\n   requirement already lives in: the same shape as `published`.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [{:keys [store address]} @published\n          result (publish/publish-module!\n                   store (safepoint/module-spec (registry) address))]\n      {:store store, :result result, :address (:address result)})))\n\n\n(defn source\n  \"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` defaults\n   to `{py <address>}`, derivation `:trusted`, the test being the\n   publisher. `pysp`-serving sources pass their own `name-env`, the\n   safepointed runs' `{'py a :pysp b}` both.\"\n  ([] (source {'py (:address @published)}))\n  ([name-env]\n   (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\n                      :name-env name-env,\n                      :derivation :trusted})))\n\n\n(defn safepoint-source\n  \"The serving composition over both published modules: `pysp` requires\n   `py`, so a safepointed linked run resolves both names.\"\nH-left-sha256.backup\nH-left-sha256.diff\nH-left-sha256.log\nantlr-build.log\nbaseline-pins.edn\nbaseline.edn\nbaseline.log\ndescriptor-check.log\ngenerate.log\nhandoff-check.log\nhandoff-regen.log\njing-quick-unrestricted.log\njing-quick.log\nlinker-r.edn\nlint.log\nmanifests.log\nmutants-summary.log\nold-debruijn-code.cljc\nold-handoff-v2.txt\nold-register-code.cljc\npack.log\npy-manifest.edn\npysp-manifest.edn\nregen-1.edn\nregen-1.err\nregen-2.edn\nregen-2.err\nregen-base.clj\nregister-row-sha256.backup\nregister-row-sha256.diff\nregister-row-sha256.log\nreplacements.edn\nstale-H-pin.backup\nstale-H-pin.diff\nstale-H-pin.log\ntouched-quick.log\ntouched-slow.log\nvm-quick-unrestricted.log\nvm-quick.log\nwrong-algorithm-pin.backup\nwrong-algorithm-pin.diff\nwrong-algorithm-pin.log\n4:   this is the publisher of `py` and `pysp`, so it publishes each module\n8:   evidence is `publish-module!`'s own `link-local` links, which\n31:    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n42:(defn registry\n45:   `stream` — the hook module `pysp` polls a signal stream, so the\n56:(def published\n57:  \"`py` published once into its own content store:\n58:   `{:store s :result r :address a}`, `r` `publish-module!`'s answer.\"\n61:          result (publish/publish-module! store\n66:(def published-pysp\n67:  \"`pysp` published once into `py`'s own content store, which its pinned\n68:   requirement already lives in: the same shape as `published`.\"\n70:    (let [{:keys [store address]} @published\n71:          result (publish/publish-module!\n76:(defn source\n77:  \"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` defaults\n79:   publisher. `pysp`-serving sources pass their own `name-env`, the\n80:   safepointed runs' `{'py a :pysp b}` both.\"\n81:  ([] (source {'py (:address @published)}))\n83:   (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\n88:(defn safepoint-source\n89:  \"The serving composition over both published modules: `pysp` requires\n92:  (source {'py (:address @published), 'pysp (:address @published-pysp)}))\n95:(defn publish-guest-module!\n96:  \"One guest module's `spec` (lower/module-spec's) published into `py`'s\n98:   `publish-module!`'s answer. Guest modules are tiny beside `py`, so a\n99:   test may publish its own; publish imports bottom-up, the `deps` map\n102:  (publish/publish-module! (:store @published) spec))\n149:(defn composition\n161:(defn drive\n174:(defn run-linked\n179:   `pysp`'s signal stream among them). A refused link throws as the task's\n191:(defn runners-under\n12:        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\n13:        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\n14:        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}\n20: ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\n21: ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\n23:         \"build/antlr/python3/classes\"]\n76:           ;; The tool version must equal the antlr4-runtime above.\n77:           :antlr-gen {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n78:                       :extra-deps {org.antlr/antlr4 {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}}\n79:                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"yang-antlr-gen\"]}\n2c70bec18b0831080375db932b324d478f14918a\n2c70bec1 docs(orchestrator-log): semantic-register-vm seat handoff, phase 7 landed, phase 8 slices 8ab and 8d in flight\n67b98907 Merge branch 'srvm-phase7': custody on rows, the rows inspector, the holder's export and recovery format 2, composition and fencing over row packs, Stage E over the semantic-register kernel, phase 7 documentation\n00243b57 fix(yin.vm.ucf): a non-BLAKE3 carrier row id is refused undecodable, the install image negative isolated (phase 7 merge review)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
